terpenoid":true,"consumer_summary":{"model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","generated":"2026-09-11","what_it_is":"Beta-copaene is a bridged sesquiterpene, a type of complex aromatic molecule built by plants and fungi to interact with their environments. While its exact aroma profile is part of a broader botanical bouquet, compounds in this class typically contribute woody, spicy, or subtly citrusy notes to the plants that produce them. You are most likely to encounter it when peeling a mandarin or crushing fresh lemon balm leaves.","found_in":"It is naturally found in a variety of familiar fruits and herbs, including pineapples, lemon balm, and several types of citrus like mandarins, pomelos, and bitter oranges. It is also a structural component of the resins in copaiba trees and larch conifers.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are primarily drawn to beta-copaene to understand how plants and fungi synthesize such intricate, multi-ringed structures. Much of the current literature focuses on the genetics and enzymes—like sesquiterpene synthases—that organisms use to build these volatile organic compounds. While biological applications are still in the very early stages, with the strongest evidence being animal and bench research (Oxford level 5), scientists are highly curious about its ecological roles, including how it interacts with insects.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"Early animal and bench work points toward beta-copaene having an effect on insect behavior, potentially acting as a botanical repellent or lure.","evidence_level":"animal","basis":"This rests on a single study evaluating its interaction with insects in an animal model.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}],"paper_count":1}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","area":"Antimicrobial activity","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether beta-copaene, like many plant volatiles, might help defend against certain bacteria or fungi.","evidence_level":"none_found","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A standardized in vitro broth microdilution assay testing isolated beta-copaene against a panel of common human and plant pathogens to establish baseline minimum inhibitory concentrations (MIC).","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000205","label":"curator inference"},"oxford":{"level":null,"label":"no evidence found"},"evidence_lines":[]},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","area":"Cellular protection","hypothesis":"Scientists are curious if this complex phytochemical can help neutralize free radicals and protect cells from oxidative stress.","evidence_level":"none_found","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"An in vitro DPPH or ORAC assay to measure the direct free-radical scavenging ability of purified beta-copaene compared to known botanical antioxidants.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000205","label":"curator inference"},"oxford":{"level":null,"label":"no evidence found"},"evidence_lines":[]},{"claim_id":"insect","area":"Agricultural pest management","hypothesis":"If beta-copaene reliably influences insect behavior, it could be developed into targeted, plant-derived agricultural lures or repellents.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A controlled field trial testing beta-copaene-baited traps against standard commercial lures to measure its efficacy in managing specific agricultural pests like fruit flies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}]}],"associated_conditions":[],"cautions":null,"evidence_at_a_glance":"The best evidence so far is bench and animal research (Oxford level 5), as most studies focus on plant genetics and biosynthesis rather than human health."}}
